<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Din Eidyn</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Eidyn</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Din Eidyn was the Brittonic name of the fortress on the rock of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a>, the chief stronghold of the post-Roman kingdom of Gododdin. The Welsh poem Y Gododdin describes its war band feasting there before the doomed attack on Catraeth. The Northumbrians besieged it in 638, and it later passed into English and then Scottish hands.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/din-eidyn.jpg" alt="Din Eidyn" width="320" height="383" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: myself · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yr.Hen.Ogledd.550.650.Koch.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The name is Brittonic, meaning the dun or hillfort of Eidyn, a district that took in at least the fortress and perhaps much of the land south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firth-of-forth">Firth of Forth</a>; Carriden, eighteen miles west, may preserve it as Caer Eidyn, though Kenneth H. Jackson held that Eidyn meant the fortress alone. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> hill forts stood at Arthur's Seat, Castle Rock and other hilltops, and archaeologists generally identify Castle Rock as Din Eidyn, since it was an important centre continuously from Roman times into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Castle Rock has been inhabited since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>, perhaps the longest nearly continuous occupation of any site in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, though it began as a minor fortified village beside others. Its defensible position gave it an advantage over Arthur's Seat. In the Roman era it held a prosperous settlement of the Votadini, probably with a hillfort, but was dwarfed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/traprain-law">Traprain Law</a>. After Traprain Law was abandoned early in the 5th century, Din Eidyn became the chief political centre of the Gododdin kingdom.</p><p>Y Gododdin tells of 300 warriors from across the Brittonic world gathering there to feast before a raid on Catraeth, probably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catterick">Catterick</a>, around 600; Ifor Williams read Mynydawc Eidyn as the ruler who summoned them, while John Koch and Graham Isaac read the name as a mountain. By the 7th century the lords of Din Eidyn may have held only their own district. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annals-of-ulster">Annals of Ulster</a> record the siege of Etin in 638, after which the fortress stayed with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angles">Angles</a> for most of three centuries. The Chronicle of the Kings of Alba says the Scots took it under King Indulf, who ruled from 954 to 962.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Castle Rock is the site of Edinburgh Castle. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angles">Angles</a> replaced the Brittonic din with the Old English burh, giving Edinburgh, and the name became Dùn Èideann in Scottish Gaelic. In Welsh tradition Eidyn long remained the northern frontier of the Britons, where Arthur's warriors fought on the Mountain of Eidyn.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Eidyn” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its centre was <b>Din Eidyn</b>, the rock of Edinburgh, and it stretched across Lothian and the eastern Borders.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-gododdin">Kingdom of Gododdin</a></li><li><blockquote>After Roman rule ended, the Votadini developed into the Kingdom of Gododdin, centred on <b>Din Eidyn</b>, Edinburgh, whose warriors are celebrated in the Welsh poem Y Gododdin.</blockquote><a href="/state/votadini">Votadini</a></li></ul></section>
